WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.105
A payments table has grown too big for one machine, so it's split across four shards.

2
00:00:08.105 --> 00:00:11.094
Each payment's id decides which shard holds it.

3
00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:12.875
How should the ids be split?

4
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.530
The simplest way is by range.

5
00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:21.644
Shard 0 holds ids 0 to 4,999, shard 1 the next five thousand, and so on.

6
00:00:21.644 --> 00:00:26.740
The router keeps the boundaries and finds where an id falls.

7
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.909
Neighbours stay together.

8
00:00:28.909 --> 00:00:36.300
A scan from payment 12,000 to 12,050 goes to shard 2 alone, which reads those rows in order.

9
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:38.151
But ids only grow.

10
00:00:38.151 --> 00:00:45.824
Each new payment gets a bigger id than the last: 20,000, then 20,001, then 20,002.

11
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:51.232
Every one is past the last boundary, so every one lands on shard 3.

12
00:00:51.232 --> 00:00:57.012
Of the next 10,000 writes, shard 3 takes all 10,000, and the other three take none.

13
00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:58.378
That's a hot spot.

14
00:00:58.378 --> 00:01:05.055
And splitting shard 3 doesn't help: its upper half becomes the new last shard, and takes every new write.

15
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:06.989
Hashing goes the other way.

16
00:01:06.989 --> 00:01:12.841
The router turns each id into a big number that looks random, and takes that number mod 4.

17
00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:20.087
Payment 20,000 lands on shard 0, 20,001 on shard 2, and 20,002 on shard 1.

18
00:01:20.087 --> 00:01:22.744
Neighbours end up far apart.

19
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:27.804
Of the next 10,000 writes, each shard takes about 2,500.

20
00:01:27.804 --> 00:01:29.283
The load is even.

21
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:31.391
But the range scan breaks.

22
00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:40.125
Payments 12,000 to 12,050 now sit on every shard, and the router can't tell which shards to skip, so it asks all four.

23
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.882
With ranges, that scan asked one shard.

24
00:01:42.882 --> 00:01:47.119
With a hash, it asks every shard, and waits for the slowest one.

25
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:50.043
So choose by the query that must be cheap.

26
00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:54.121
Ranges keep neighbours together, but pile new keys on one shard.

27
00:01:54.121 --> 00:01:57.435
A hash spreads the writes, but scatters every range.
